  "text": "Let's approach this problem methodically. First, notice that a right triangle has two legs (sides perpendicular to each other) and one hypotenuse. Here, we're looking for triangles with a side length of 2 units, which is the length of the legs, and a hypotenuse that is not parallel to any side of the geoboard. This means the hypotenuse must be longer than 2 units (sides of 2 units) to form a right triangle and not equal to any of the 1x1 grid lines (which would make the sides parallel).\n\nLet's denote the right triangle as having legs of length 2 units. By the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n\\[ \\text{hypotenuse}^2 = 2^2 + 2^2 = 8 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{hypotenuse} = \\sqrt{8} = 2\\sqrt{2} \\approx 2.828 \\]\n\nSince the hypotenuse must be an integer or a multiple of the grid unit (1 unit), which is 2.828 not matching, we conclude our hypotenuse must be longer than 2 units and less than 3 units as the hypotenuse could approximately equal 3 if the legs were somewhat longer at around the 1-squared bordering the integer values.\n\nNow, considering right triangles with hypotenuse 3: In a 3-4-5 triangle, both 3 and 4 are not 2 units, but only 4 and 3. And considering triangle conditions follow 2 units length always satisfy 3 incident combinations order irrespective of angle with vertices on grid square corners or boundary edges of 1x1 square units to generate solutions:\n\n\\[ \\text{isosceles configurations replicated and ensuring whole sides only} \\]\n\n\\[ (2,2)\\rightarrow (\\text{by parallel geometry and scaling}) ]\n\n\\[ \\text{isosceles pairs}=\\text{permutations of} \\Rightarrow \\dots \\]\n\nHence, no configurations make right ab and more importantly satisfy hypotenuse criteria more toward corners or extremities:\n\n\\[ iso\\rightarrow wave marginale or correct: \\]\n\n\\[ \\boxed{416 \\ points\\ refined\\ or\\) : valid configurations determine triangles rightedge or edge away } .",
